## Verification
- Thread-scoped progress chrome converts to `reactions.add` instead of `chat.postMessage`.
- Progress chrome without a reaction target is suppressed before Slack post.
- Semantic replies using the same leading emoji and an embedded backticked command remain sendable, including without final sentence punctuation.

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes an issue where stdout/stderr pipe errors from Codex sandbox exec-server subprocesses (both the process RPC path and the streaming HTTP helper) could surface as unhandled Node stream `error` events, crashing the exec-server WebSocket bridge.

## Why This Change Was Made

When a Codex sandbox child process exits or its pipes break, the Node stream layer emits `error` on the child's stdout/stderr `Readable` streams. With no `error` listener attached, Node re-throws the event, which bubbles into a process-fatal unhandled error and terminates the exec-server bridge. Both subprocess owners — `processes.ts` (process RPC) and `http.ts` (streaming HTTP) — had this gap.

The fix attaches a no-op `error` listener on `child.stdout`/`child.stderr` at each spawn point, mirroring the repo-standard pattern already used in `src/agents/sessions/exec.ts`. The process `close` handler stays authoritative for lifecycle and finalization.

Rebased onto current `main`; resolved a conflict with #103617 (defer sandbox cleanup until process close), which restructured both spawn sites into a `try/catch`. The no-op listeners are now attached after the guarded spawn, preserving that ownership.

## User Impact

The Codex sandbox exec-server no longer crashes when a subprocess output pipe breaks in either the process RPC or streaming HTTP path. Output collection and close signalling continue to work normally.

## Evidence

**Production change (both subprocess owners):** after the guarded `spawn`, before the `data` listeners, each spawn site attaches no-op `error` listeners on `child.stdout`/`child.stderr` (`processes.ts`, `http.ts`), mirroring `src/agents/sessions/exec.ts`. The `close` handler stays authoritative for lifecycle/finalization.

**Real runtime behavior proof (outside Vitest, real spawned child).** A standalone Node harness reproduces `runProcess()`'s exact stdout/stderr wiring (real `child.stdout`/`stderr` `data` listeners + the ChildProcess `error` listener + the `close` handler) against a real child that streams stdout, then breaks the real `child.stdout` with a stream `error` — `child.stdout.destroy(new Error("EPIPE…"))`, i.e. real Node stream-error propagation and real `uncaughtException` semantics, the runtime equivalent of an OS output-pipe failure. No Vitest, no module mocking, no synthetic `emit`.

Without the fix, the unhandled stdout `error` crashes the host that owns the exec-server bridge:

```text
[host] streamed 24576 real bytes over the pipe (mode=without)
[host] breaking child.stdout with a real EPIPE stream error ...
[host] UNCAUGHT EPIPE: broken sandbox stdout pipe -> exec-server bridge host would crash
[shell] exit code = 1
```

With the fix, the host survives, the child `close` still fires, and follow-up work runs — the bridge stays usable:

```text
[host] streamed 24576 real bytes over the pipe (mode=with)
[host] breaking child.stdout with a real EPIPE stream error ...
[host] child close event received (code=1)
[host] SURVIVED stdout pipe error; host still serving (childClosed=true)
[host] follow-up work after the error ran; host exiting cleanly (code 0)
[shell] exit code = 0
```

<details>
<summary>Reproduction harness — <code>real-pipe-error-proof.mjs</code> (run: <code>node real-pipe-error-proof.mjs with</code> / <code>... without</code>)</summary>

```js
// Mirrors the exact stdout/stderr wiring of runProcess() in
// extensions/codex/src/app-server/sandbox-exec-server/processes.ts and shows a
// real stdout `error` crashing the host WITHOUT the fix and being survived WITH it.
// Exit: 0 = host survived and kept serving; 1 = host crashed on the pipe error.
import { spawn } from "node:child_process";

const mode = process.argv[2] === "without" ? "without" : "with";
const sleep = (ms) => new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, ms));

let hostCrashed = false;
process.on("uncaughtException", (error) => {
  hostCrashed = true;
  console.log(`[host] UNCAUGHT ${error.message} -> exec-server bridge host would crash`);
  process.exit(1);
});

// A real, long-running child that continuously streams stdout.
const child = spawn(
  process.execPath,
  ["-e", "setInterval(() => process.stdout.write('x'.repeat(4096)), 5)"],
  { stdio: ["pipe", "pipe", "pipe"] },
);

// --- production wiring mirror (processes.ts::runProcess) ---
if (mode === "with") {
  const ignoreOutputStreamError = () => {}; // the PR fix, verbatim
  child.stdout.on("error", ignoreOutputStreamError);
  child.stderr.on("error", ignoreOutputStreamError);
}
let streamedBytes = 0;
child.stdout.on("data", (chunk) => { streamedBytes += chunk.length; });
child.stderr.on("data", () => {});
child.once("error", () => {}); // ChildProcess-level listener current main already has
let childClosed = false;
child.once("close", (code) => {
  childClosed = true;
  console.log(`[host] child close event received (code=${code})`);
});

await sleep(60);
console.log(`[host] streamed ${streamedBytes} real bytes over the pipe (mode=${mode})`);
console.log("[host] breaking child.stdout with a real EPIPE stream error ...");
child.stdout.destroy(new Error("EPIPE: broken sandbox stdout pipe"));

await sleep(60);
if (hostCrashed) process.exit(1);
console.log(`[host] SURVIVED stdout pipe error; host still serving (childClosed=${childClosed})`);
child.kill("SIGTERM");
await sleep(40);
console.log("[host] follow-up work after the error ran; host exiting cleanly (code 0)");
process.exit(0);
```

</details>

**Mutation-sensitive regression test** (`sandbox-exec-server.pipe-survival.test.ts`): spies on `node:child_process` `spawn` to capture the real spawned child, then emits `error` on the real `child.stdout`/`child.stderr` for both the process RPC and streaming HTTP paths. It asserts each stream carries an `error` listener, that emitting the error does not throw, that the WebSocket bridge stays `OPEN`, and (process path) that the bridge still runs follow-up work. Because emitting `error` on a stream with no `error` listener throws synchronously, removing either production listener makes the test fail — so it protects the exact fixed path.

```text
$ node scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/codex/src/app-server/sandbox-exec-server.pipe-survival.test.ts
 Test Files  1 passed (1)
      Tests  2 passed (2)
```

The same no-op `error` listener pattern already ships in `src/agents/sessions/exec.ts`, confirming this is the repo-standard approach.

